    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Working as a patent examiner in Bern, Einstein published four papers in 1905 on the photoelectric effect, Brownian motion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">special relativity</a> and the equivalence of mass and energy, and in 1915 proposed the general theory of relativity.</li><li>Eddington's observations of a solar eclipse on 29 May 1919 were consistent with his calculations, and newspapers around the world reported them, which made Einstein, in one writer's phrase, perhaps the world's first celebrity scientist.</li><li>Driven out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by the Nazis in 1933, he settled at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, where he signed the 1939 letter to President Roosevelt on nuclear weapons and spent his last decades disputing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum mechanics</a> and seeking a unified field theory.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Einstein's doctoral dissertation of 1905, on molecular dimensions, was 24 pages long and dedicated to his friend Marcel Grossmann; he was formally awarded his PhD on 15 January 1906.</p></aside>
    </section>

