      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1879</span><span class="rf-text">Born in Ulm, in the Kingdom of Württemberg, on 14 March</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">Renounces his Württemberg citizenship on 28 January and enrols at the federal polytechnic school in Zurich</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1903</span><span class="rf-text">Marries Mileva Marić in January; the Swiss Patent Office makes his position as assistant examiner permanent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text">Publishes four papers on the photoelectric effect, Brownian motion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">special relativity</a> and the equivalence of mass and energy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">Proposes the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">theory of relativity</a>, extending his mechanics to include gravitation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Eddington's observations of the solar eclipse of 29 May give results consistent with his calculations, and the press reports them worldwide</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1933</span><span class="rf-text">Surrenders his passport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> after the Nazis take power, and in October takes up a post at the Institute for Advanced Study</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1955</span><span class="rf-text">Dies at Princeton Hospital on 18 April, aged 76</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Albert Einstein was born in Ulm, in the Kingdom of Württemberg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, on 14 March 1879. His parents, secular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi Jews</a>, were Hermann Einstein, a salesman and engineer, and Pauline Koch. In 1880 the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, where his father and his uncle Jakob founded a company that made electrical equipment based on direct current. His parents worried that he had a learning disability because he was very slow to learn to talk. When he was five and sick in bed, his father brought him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a>, which sparked a lifelong fascination with electromagnetism. He attended a Catholic elementary school from the age of five and at eight moved to the Luitpold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a>.</p><p>He began teaching himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, calculus and Euclidean geometry at twelve, and at thirteen a family tutor, Max Talmud, introduced him to Kant's Critique of Pure Reason. In 1894 his father's firm lost a bid to light Munich because it lacked the capital to change from direct to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alternating-current">alternating current</a>, and the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and then to Pavia. Einstein, then fifteen, stayed behind to finish school. He later wrote that the Gymnasium's strict rote learning was harmful to creativity, and at the end of December 1894 a doctor's letter persuaded its authorities to release him. In 1895 he failed the general part of the entrance examination for the polytechnic school in Zurich but did well in physics and mathematics, and he completed his schooling at the cantonal school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aarau">Aarau</a>, graduating in 1896.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Albert Einstein&#8221; cites where it covers Childhood, youth and education:</p><ul><li>Albrecht Fölsing, Albert Einstein (Penguin Viking, 1997)</li><li>Roger Highfield, The Private Lives of Albert Einstein (Faber and Faber, 1993)</li><li>Alice Calaprice, The New Quotable Einstein (Princeton University Press, 2005)</li><li>Abraham Pais, Einstein Lived Here (Oxford University Press, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henri-poincare"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-poincare.jpg" alt="Henri Poincaré" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henri-poincare">Henri Poincaré</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V82_D416_Henri_Poincare.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 28 January 1896, six weeks before he turned seventeen, Einstein renounced his Württemberg citizenship, which avoided conscription into military service. He then enrolled in the four-year mathematics and physics teaching diploma programme at the Zurich polytechnic school. His friend Marcel Grossmann helped him get by despite his loose study habits, and among his fellow students was Mileva Marić, with whom he spent many hours discussing physics. They became lovers; a daughter, Lieserl, was born in early 1902, and what happened to her is uncertain. They married in January 1903.</p><p>Einstein graduated in 1900 and acquired Swiss citizenship in February 1901. Swiss schools offered him no teaching post despite almost two years of applications, and with the help of Grossmann's father he secured a post at the Swiss Patent Office in Bern as an assistant examiner, level III; it was made permanent in 1903. In 1902 he and his friends Conrad Habicht and Maurice Solovine formed a discussion group, the Olympia Academy, which read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-poincare">Henri Poincaré</a>, Ernst Mach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a>. His doctoral dissertation was completed on 30 April 1905.</p><p>The academic path then opened. In 1908 he became a junior teacher at the University of Bern, in 1909 an associate professor at Zurich, in 1911 a full professor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, and in July 1912 a professor of theoretical physics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eth-zurich">ETH Zurich</a>. In 1913 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a> and Walther Nernst offered him membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Prussian Academy of Sciences</a>, the directorship of a planned physics institute and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> chair with no teaching duties, and he moved there in April 1914.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Albert Einstein&#8221; cites where it covers Marriages, relationships and children, Assistant at the Swiss Patent Office (1902–1909), First scientific papers (1900–1905), Academic career in Europe (1908–1933):</p><ul><li>Albrecht Fölsing, Albert Einstein (Penguin Viking, 1997)</li><li>Abraham Pais, Subtle is the Lord: The Science and the Life of Albert Einstein (Oxford University Press, 1982)</li><li>Alice Calaprice, The New Quotable Einstein (Princeton University Press, 2005)</li><li>David E. Rowe (ed.), Einstein on Politics: His Private Thoughts and Public Stands on Nationalism, Zionism, War, Peace, and the Bomb (Princeton University Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/max-planck"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-planck.jpg" alt="Max Planck" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Max_Planck_by_Hugo_Erfurth_1938cr_-_restoration1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugo Erfurth, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1905, sometimes described as his annus mirabilis, Einstein published four papers in the Annalen der Physik. They outlined a theory of the photoelectric effect, explained Brownian motion, introduced the special <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">theory of relativity</a> and showed that mass and energy are equivalent. In the paper on moving bodies, received on 30 June, he reconciled conflicts between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxwell-s-equations">Maxwell's equations</a> and Newtonian mechanics by changing the laws of mechanics, and argued that the luminiferous aether was superfluous. The relativity work remained controversial for many years but was accepted by leading physicists, starting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a>.</p><p>From 1907 he worked towards a new theory of gravitation, beginning with the equivalence principle. After a false start with the Entwurf theory of 1913, which he abandoned in November 1915, he completed the general theory of relativity that year, using Riemannian geometry, and applied it to the precession of Mercury's perihelion. Observations by Sir Arthur Eddington during the total eclipse of 29 May 1919 yielded results consistent with his calculations, and on 7 November 1919 The Times printed the headline "Revolution in Science – New Theory of the Universe – Newtonian Ideas Overthrown".</p><p>In 1917 he applied general relativity to the universe as a whole, introducing the cosmological constant, and wrote a paper proposing stimulated emission. In 1924 he translated and supported a paper by Satyendra Nath Bose, which led to Bose–Einstein statistics. He became the first director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> Wilhelm Institute for Physics in 1917 and received the 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics, awarded in 1922, for the law of the photoelectric effect.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/niels-bohr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niels-bohr.jpg" alt="Niels Bohr" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/niels-bohr">Niels Bohr</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niels_Bohr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The American Institute of Physics credits the photo [1] to AB Lagrelius &amp; Westphal, which is the Swedish company ..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>His light quanta were nearly universally rejected by physicists, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niels-bohr">Niels Bohr</a>, and the Nobel citation of 1922 did not assent to his notion of the particulate nature of light. After 1925 he grew displeased with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum mechanics</a> as it had evolved, objecting that God "is not playing at dice", and he maintained to the end of his life that the theory was incomplete. His public disputes with Bohr are remembered for their importance to the philosophy of science. In 1935 he published with Boris Podolsky and Nathan Rosen the paper known as the EPR paradox. In 1964 John Stewart Bell showed that hidden variables would have to be nonlocal, and said the paradox "is resolved in the way which Einstein would have liked least". His search for a unified field theory did not succeed, and he became isolated from mainstream physics.</p><p>His private life drew criticism too. In 1912 he began a relationship with Elsa Löwenthal; when Marić learned of it in 1914 she returned to Zurich with their sons, Hans Albert and Eduard, and the couple were divorced on 14 February 1919. He married Löwenthal in 1919. Eduard was diagnosed with schizophrenia and lived the rest of his life in his mother's care or in an asylum. Historians of physics are divided on how far Marić contributed to the 1905 papers.</p><p>His travel diary of 1922 recorded views of Chinese people, and of Indian people, that were uncomplimentary; of the Chinese he wrote that "even the children are spiritless and look obtuse". Some argue that signing the 1939 letter to Roosevelt went against his pacifist principles. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> the Nazis targeted his works in the book burnings of 1933, and one magazine listed him as an enemy of the regime, "not yet hanged", with a bounty on his head.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="Winston Churchill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Winston_Churchill_-_19086236948_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In February 1933, while visiting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Einstein knew he could not return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> came to power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> repeatedly raided his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> apartment, and the Nazis raided his cottage, confiscated his sailboat and later converted the cottage into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitler-youth">Hitler Youth</a> camp. On landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> on 28 March he surrendered his passport at the German consulate; the Nazi government revoked his citizenship on 24 March 1934. He stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> and others to help bring Jewish scientists out of Germany. His letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">İsmet İnönü</a> in September 1933 led to invitations to over 1,000 people. In October he took up a position at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, which he held until his death. Elsa died in December 1936, and he became an American citizen in 1940.</p><p>In July 1939 Leó Szilárd and Eugene Wigner visited him to explain the possibility of atomic bombs, and he signed a letter with Szilárd to President Roosevelt recommending that the United States begin its own nuclear weapons research. In 1954 he told Linus Pauling that signing it was "one great mistake in my life", though there was some justification. In 1955 he and ten others signed a manifesto on the danger of nuclear weapons.</p><p>In 1952, after Chaim Weizmann died, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a> offered him the presidency of Israel, which he declined. On 17 April 1955 an abdominal aortic aneurysm ruptured; he refused surgery, saying "It is tasteless to prolong life artificially", and died early the next morning at the age of 76. A pathologist removed his brain without the family's permission. His ashes were scattered at an undisclosed location.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Einstein left his papers and literary estate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which he had helped to establish. The release of more than 30,000 unique documents was announced in 2014. In his final four years he was also involved in founding the Albert Einstein College of Medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, and the element einsteinium was named in his honour in 1955.</p><p>Several of his predictions were confirmed long after he made them. Gravitational waves, predicted in 1916, were first observed directly by researchers at LIGO, who published their result on 11 February 2016, and a Bose–Einstein condensate was first produced experimentally in 1995. His 1917 paper on stimulated emission describes the process that makes the laser and maser possible. The EPR paper, which Einstein himself found overly complicated, became among the most influential papers published in Physical Review and is considered a centrepiece of the development of quantum information theory. Most researchers now believe his approach to unifying physics was mistaken, but his goal of a theory of everything is one to which his successors still aspire.</p><p>The Times and other newspapers made him an intellectual icon after 1919, and his name became synonymous with genius in popular culture. The physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer, speaking at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1965, remembered in him "a wonderful purity at once childlike and profoundly stubborn". Einstein described himself as a "deeply religious nonbeliever" and said he had sympathy for the impersonal God of Spinoza.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Albert Einstein (1879–1955), born in Ulm and later a Swiss and American citizen, published four papers in 1905 while a patent examiner and proposed the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">theory of relativity</a> in 1915. Forced out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1933, he settled at Princeton, where he disputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum mechanics</a>.</em></p>

